{:docker-login:}[registry login]

* Start a Compose project
Run beside `compose.yaml`. Replace `app` below with a service name from that file.
@code bash
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose ps
@end
Configuration validation does not check whether the application itself starts successfully.

* Follow recent logs
@code bash
docker compose logs --tail=100 -f app
@end
Ctrl-C stops following the logs; the service keeps running.

* Open a shell in a running service
@code bash
docker compose exec app sh
@end
The image must contain a shell; minimal images may not include one.

* Inspect containers and mounts
Replace `container_name` with a name from `docker ps -a`.
@code bash
docker ps -a
docker inspect --format '{{json .Mounts}}' container_name
docker volume ls
docker volume inspect volume_name
@end

* Stop services or remove the project containers
@code bash
docker compose stop
# To remove containers and the project network instead:
docker compose down
@end
Named volumes remain by default. `docker compose down --volumes` deletes the project's declared named volumes and attached anonymous volumes, so use it only when that data is disposable. External volumes remain.

Related: {:kubectl:}[kubectl]. Reference: {https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/}[Compose commands].
